WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.083 --> 00:00:02.916
(intro music)

00:00:02.916 --> 00:00:03.749
(audience applauds)

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.360
- Have y'all ever met before?

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.280
- We met very briefly at a movie

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.049
that she was in that was so
good earlier this summer.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:13.882
- Yeah.

00:00:13.882 --> 00:00:15.690
- So your new song is called Bad Idea.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.150
So what it's what It's
obviously about a bad idea.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:18.983
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:20.649
Yeah, whoa.
- Well I was gonna ask you,

00:00:20.649 --> 00:00:22.020
but I think it's probably
about a bad idea.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:22.853
- Yeah.

00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:23.880
- This is so cool.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:24.713
- Thank you!

00:00:24.713 --> 00:00:25.680
- All the artistic stuff that you do.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.648
- Thanks, yeah.

00:00:26.648 --> 00:00:27.481
- Yes!

00:00:27.481 --> 00:00:28.586
- No, I know, we were,

00:00:28.586 --> 00:00:30.360
we were in Paris and we were

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.670
feeling the fashion week energy.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.213
And so we had these bleached
brows, which people are very

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:37.680
very mixed reviews on these bleach brows.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:38.513
Yeah. But

00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:39.411
- Like, I hate it.

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:40.380
You look like that.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.830
You can do anything you want.

00:00:43.386 --> 00:00:45.090
Oh, so your single boyfriend came.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.050
It became an anthem for
LGBTQ+ community, all of us.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.000
I love the song, thought was so clever.

00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:54.043
Did you write that for you, really?

00:00:54.043 --> 00:00:56.190
Or did you write that
just because of a situ?

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.081
Like why did, what, why
was that in your head?

00:00:58.081 --> 00:01:02.460
- Yeah, honestly, it's
funny, it's, I wrote it based

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.330
on a really, kind of like,
rough experience that I had

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.310
that I would never,
like if you told me then

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.750
that I was gonna write
that song about that night

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.070
it's like so incongruous

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.760
and, kind of like, very
different energies.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.140
And I ended up with a sort
of a, like a perspective

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.360
that I could sit with, right?

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.680
Yeah. Which is that, like, instead of,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.152
instead of facing the fact
of, like, someone being a very

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.990
harmful person in my life, it was,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.140
I could be a better boyfriend

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.960
than this situation that was
going on that I was, you know,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.541
a part of or witnessing.

00:01:35.541 --> 00:01:37.440
And I really never

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.000
I never thought I was
gonna play it for anybody.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.170
I never thought anybody was gonna hear it.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.510
I was just like, I think
this kind of smacks.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.049
So I was like, I play this.

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:45.847
- Oh my God, I love it.

00:01:45.847 --> 00:01:46.830
I literally the first time I heard it.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:47.907
I listened, like, 20 times in a row.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.310
I was like, "This is a jam!"

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.143
- Thank you.

00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:51.976
- I love it.

00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:52.809
It's so good.

00:01:52.809 --> 00:01:53.642
- Oh, thank you.

00:01:53.642 --> 00:01:55.077
- How do you feel like
it may have changed you?

00:01:55.077 --> 00:01:56.880
Do you feel like, 'cause
sometimes I feel like,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.220
one, it's important to
write and that's my vessel

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.330
of like therapy almost.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.810
But then, you get to perform
it all the time and then

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.633
it like carries that place with you.

00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:07.830
That can be hard.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.800
Does it, do you keep that
or does it, or do you change

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.633
and you grow after that?

00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:13.650
Sometimes it's hard to re-sing something.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:14.483
You're like, ugh.

00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:17.730
- No, I would imagine
as well, you know, a lot

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.580
of your music is so, you're
so, you're very emotive,

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.413
you know.

00:02:21.413 --> 00:02:22.650
It's very story based, right?

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.483
Narrative.

00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:26.123
I definitely find,
specifically with boyfriend,

00:02:26.123 --> 00:02:28.500
because it was such a
huge part of me finding my

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.290
own identity and embracing my queerness.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.300
And it, like, makes me emotional
every time I talk about it.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:38.006
But it was such a, like,
a key to a gate for me,

00:02:38.006 --> 00:02:41.910
into stepping into myself wholly
in a way that I never knew

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.520
if I was gonna be privileged enough

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.205
to find those pieces of
myself and, and exist

00:02:47.205 --> 00:02:48.450
as that person, right?

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.283
'Cause that's like the fear.

00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:50.940
The fear is that you
exist as yourself wholly,

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.830
and the world cracks
open, and everything ends,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.224
and all the lights go out.

00:02:54.224 --> 00:02:57.420
And when the song came out,
and when I saw the people

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.904
were emotionally connecting to it,

00:02:58.904 --> 00:03:01.350
I think I was really
panicked for some reason,

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.550
just cause I wasn't expecting it.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.383
- Yeah.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.697
- And then over time,
it was like, it melded

00:03:05.697 --> 00:03:09.150
into this gorgeous, like
something meaningful

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.310
for me being something
meaningful for others.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.206
I'm sure you've had that before.

00:03:13.206 --> 00:03:14.039
- Yeah.

00:03:14.039 --> 00:03:15.090
- It's, it becomes this
like, especially when

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.490
it's something about, you know, identity,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.646
and queerness, and how
people view themselves being.

00:03:19.646 --> 00:03:20.479
- Something so personal and intimate.

00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:23.190
- And finding empowerment in
it when it's something that,

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.780
you know, can sometimes feel very heavy

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.185
to carry alone, identity.

00:03:26.185 --> 00:03:27.018
- Yeah.

00:03:27.018 --> 00:03:29.315
- And I've found it incredibly
special, and meaningful,

00:03:29.315 --> 00:03:31.230
and such a surprise.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.280
Such a beautiful surprise.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.620
- Yeah. I like taking any kind of negative

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.366
and making a positive out of it.

00:03:36.366 --> 00:03:37.199
- Yeah.

00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:38.865
- And possibly making
some money off that too.

00:03:38.865 --> 00:03:40.710
So, I mean, you're a writer as well.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:41.910
You get that too, like in a sense

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.670
of like relating to them and
connecting to them in that way.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.270
- I think with writing, for
like, particularly the cast

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.760
of College Girls, they're so
funny, and they're so great,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.100
and you get rewrite the narrative.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.460
Like I get to write these
four girls and show how

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.950
they relate, and how they
get over their issues,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.686
but can come together even
though they're so different.

00:04:00.686 --> 00:04:03.457
They're like socioeconomically different,

00:04:03.457 --> 00:04:04.593
like, racially different.

00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:07.680
And that you can make it so
that it's like harmonious,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.513
and they're friends.

00:04:08.513 --> 00:04:10.890
And, like, I think sometimes
when you create those things

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.660
then you allow other people
to see them, and be like,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.280
oh it, that is possible, right?

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.120
- Totally.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.190
- And they're so talented,
these four actresses.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.220
And like, I just, I
learned so much from them.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.560
And sometimes, we're
conditioned as writers to think

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.360
about people who are older
than us who came before us

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.130
and say like, "Hey, they inspire me,"

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.740
but I really am inspired
by the young women

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.137
that I work with, you know, every day.

00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:31.650
And it makes me a better writer.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.483
- I know.

00:04:32.483 --> 00:04:34.950
I feel like a lot of
people will, and I'm 40,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:35.783
I don't know how old you are.

00:04:35.783 --> 00:04:37.905
I'm not gonna ask you,
but I just mean like,

00:04:37.905 --> 00:04:38.850
I feel like in my generation,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:39.683
I feel like a lot of people are like,

00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:41.785
"Oh, the young generation
is so apathetic," or

00:04:41.785 --> 00:04:43.770
"I'm worried about it," or whatever.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.102
And I'm like, I actually,
having this show specifically,

00:04:46.102 --> 00:04:49.650
like, I've seen so many
young people, like,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.200
they're more, I mean, I don't know.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.660
I feel like they're advocates,
like, far more than I was

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:55.493
at their age.

00:04:55.493 --> 00:04:56.326
- Passion, on 100.

00:04:56.326 --> 00:04:57.576
- And more empathy.

00:04:57.576 --> 00:05:00.840
It's a different kind of generation,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.030
and it's actually, I think hopeful.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.040
Like, for the people that I encounter

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.333
that are a different generation than me,

00:05:06.333 --> 00:05:07.380
that are younger.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.677
- I feel like I always
went into it being like,

00:05:08.677 --> 00:05:10.620
"I don't wanna be one of those
older people that's always

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.000
complaining about the younger people."

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.100
And I find myself doing
that a lot, you know?

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:14.933
- Yeah.

00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:15.780
- 'Cause, it's just nature.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.130
Like, you just get annoyed at people.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.600
You think it was easier
for them or whatever,

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.430
but one thing that is undeniable about,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.900
at least the young women
I work with and see,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.566
is how they're unafraid to be activists.

00:05:24.566 --> 00:05:27.000
When I was coming up, I was
like, I don't want anyone to

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.940
think I'm like so political,
or self confident,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.216
or think I demand rights and
all this privilege, you know?

00:05:32.216 --> 00:05:34.230
- Oh my God, girl, I'm
from the south, yeah.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.260
- Yeah, and so now to see
young women who are, like,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.240
unafraid to do that,
especially in Hollywood,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.940
it's like very, it's very refreshing.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.533
But then also, you know,
makes you think like, "Oh,

00:05:44.533 --> 00:05:46.950
I could have done a better
job when I was younger.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:47.783
- I know.

00:05:47.783 --> 00:05:48.750
But you know what it is
though, like what you said,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.400
that's what's so cool, is
that you're setting this up

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.406
for even this generation
to, 'cause these are

00:05:52.406 --> 00:05:55.650
applicable things to their, to
these people in their lives.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.570
Like you just said, you're
bringing people together

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.430
that maybe wouldn't normally
be brought together, especially

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:00.526
on TV, but maybe even in life still.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.366
And then, maybe there's
people in college going,

00:06:02.366 --> 00:06:03.900
"That's kinda like my group."

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.987
Or like, "This could be my group."

00:06:04.987 --> 00:06:06.180
"I wanna find that group."

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.540
I love how writing and
creativity and art can do that.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.490
- I mean, there's nothing I
feel like more influential

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.190
than storytelling in
like, any form, right?

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.410
It's like, you know,
songwriting, TV, film,

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.440
like there was nothing
that I learned more from

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.400
than those mediums.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:21.660
Which is why I think we're all here.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.493
So, you know.

00:06:22.493 --> 00:06:23.730
- Yeah. They're powerful.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:24.900
Well, we have a surprise for you.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.730
- You have a surprise for me?
- Yes, we do that.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:27.563
- What does that mean?

00:06:27.563 --> 00:06:30.120
- In honor of over 1 million records sold,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.890
man, we're excited to present you with

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.450
the R-I-double-A platinum edition

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.091
for your new single "Boyfriend."

00:06:35.091 --> 00:06:37.807
(audience applauds)

00:06:37.807 --> 00:06:41.550
- Thank you so much.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:42.390
- I was so excited when
we out you getting it,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.013
I was like, "I wanna give it to her."

00:06:44.013 --> 00:06:46.560
Dove's latest single is called Bad Idea,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.480
and it's available wherever
you get your music.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.970
Check it out, y'all.

